WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.234
Upload workflows should control where files are stored from the beginning.

2
00:00:04.234 --> 00:00:14.621
The application should choose the destination, generate or approve the
storage name, apply size limits, capture safe metadata, and avoid trusting
the user-provided filename as a path.

3
00:00:14.621 --> 00:00:19.847
Content type and extension checks can support policy, but they should not
be the only control.

4
00:00:19.847 --> 00:00:25.735
Upload handling should assume filenames and metadata are untrusted even
when the user is authenticated.

5
00:00:25.735 --> 00:00:29.308
Download endpoints require authorization before file access.

6
00:00:29.308 --> 00:00:35.924
The application should verify the current subject, action, resource, and
context before opening or serving content.

7
00:00:35.924 --> 00:00:46.046
A route that accepts a file identifier should confirm that the file
exists, belongs to the expected tenant or organization, is in a
downloadable state, and is available to the caller.

8
00:00:46.046 --> 00:00:51.338
Hiding links in the user interface does not replace server-side object-
level access control.

9
00:00:51.338 --> 00:00:54.911
Tenant and organization boundaries are central to file safety.

10
00:00:54.911 --> 00:01:01.130
In multi-tenant systems, a correct path operation can still be a security
failure if it returns another tenant?

11
00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:02.188
s file.

12
00:01:02.188 --> 00:01:10.127
Storage records should include ownership or tenancy information, and
access checks should use that information close to the file operation.

13
00:01:10.127 --> 00:01:17.537
Background jobs, exports, previews, thumbnails, and generated reports
should follow the same boundary rules as direct downloads.

14
00:01:17.537 --> 00:01:22.896
File serving should use framework or storage helpers deliberately when
they match the use case.

15
00:01:22.896 --> 00:01:33.216
Helpers can handle common response behavior, headers, streaming, range
requests, and safe storage integration, but they do not automatically
solve authorization or storage design.

16
00:01:33.216 --> 00:01:37.120
Avoid exposing internal paths in responses, logs, or error messages.

17
00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:42.280
A safe download tells the user what they are allowed to receive, not how
the server is laid out.
